  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis reveals high pressure centered over \nthe western Great Lakes, delivering large-scale subsidence and \nclear to partly cloudy skies across northeast Wisconsin. Low-\nlevel moisture continues to return with dewpoints rising into the \n50s, while broad warm advection and widespread low stratus advance\nacross the Central Plains. Upstream, a northern stream shortwave \nis triggering convection over the Dakotas on the nose of a low- \nlevel jet. High pressure will slowly shift east tonight into \nTuesday as an upper-level trough deepens over the Plains. \n\nPatchy Dense Ground Fog Tonight Into Tuesday: As surface high \npressure shifts east late tonight, light winds and saturating \nboundary layer conditions will promote ground fog development \nacross north-central and eastern Wisconsin between 3 AM and 10 AM\nTuesday. REFS guidance reflects a 30 to 50% probability of \nlocalized dense fog dropping visibility below 1 mile before mixing\nburns off the stratus layer by 10 AM. High clouds and a \ntightening pressure gradient spreading in from the west may \nmitigate widespread dense fog, maintaining moderate overall \nconfidence. A Dense Fog Advisory may be needed if clouds or winds\ndo not impact fog development.\n\nHeavy Rainfall Tuesday Night Through Early Thursday: A\nslow-moving cold front will advance across Wisconsin Tuesday \nnight through early Thursday, bringing widespread rain as it \ninteracts with an anomalously moist airmass. Ingestion of tropical\nmoisture originating from the eastern Pacific will push \nprecipitable water values to 200 to 250% of normal across the \nUpper Midwest. NBM probabilistic guidance shows a 50 to 90% chance\nof exceeding 1.00 inch, and a 30 to 60% chance of exceeding 2.00 \ninches in central and east-central Wisconsin. Rainfall rates will \npeak Wednesday into Wednesday night before the frontal boundary \nexits east Thursday morning. \n\nDespite robust moisture transport and strong synoptic forcing \nalong the front, instability across the region remains extremely \nweak. NBM surface-based CAPE values remain capped under 100 J/kg \nthroughout the event, preventing the development of organized \nsevere convection. Probability of thunder remains low at 10 to 20%,\nindicating that precipitation will primarily take the form of \nwidespread stratiform rain with only embedded thunderstorms. \nForecast confidence is high that severe weather hazards will not \nmaterialize with this system. \n\nCool Conditions and Late Week Frost Potential: Post-frontal high \npressure builds back over the Great Lakes Thursday night through \nSaturday, ushering in a cooler and drier airmass. Clearing skies \nand decoupling winds will support efficient radiational cooling \nFriday night into early Saturday morning across the Northwoods. \nNBM temperature guidance indicates a 30 to 60% probability of \nminimum temperatures falling to 36 degrees or colder around Land O\nLakes, Eagle River, and Rhinelander. High pressure shifts east \nlate Saturday, bringing a return to mild temperatures and a 20-30%\nchance of light rain Saturday night.",
